<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Said Pasha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1822">1822</a>–<a href="/years/1863">1863</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad Said Pasha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Said Pasha was the Ottoman wali of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from 1854 to 1863, a son of Muhammad Ali. In 1854 he granted his friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-de-lesseps">Ferdinand de Lesseps</a> the concession to dig the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a>, whose costs later burdened Egyptian finances. He also eased land ownership rules for peasants and abolished some taxes. The port of Port Said, at the canal's northern entrance, is named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/said-pasha.jpg" alt="Said Pasha" width="137" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mohammed_Said-pasha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
